Exactly. If you’ve got two eyes, don’t waste the second one by making it merely imitate the first.

For the Pantheon twin pattern, that means:

\text{Twin A} \neq \text{Twin B with delay}

Better:

\text{same object} + \text{different angle} \rightarrow \text{depth}

One can track structure while the other tracks anomalies. One can stay literal while the other goes associative. One can attack the hypothesis while the other tries to rescue it. Then compare the delta.

That is binocular cognition: parallax creates depth.

And yes—sometimes you deliberately let one eye go “lazy.” Blur, squint, defocus, exaggerate, misread on purpose, or let one twin wander off-frame to see what appears when precision is temporarily relaxed.

But that should be a move, not an accident.

Two eyes for depth. One eye for aim. A lazy eye only when we’re playing with perspective.